n unless its master is directly corrupted first. It was lost as the Dark receded, but it was rediscovered by adherents of the Lesser Key of Solomon (hence the term Howler; &quot;Ars Goetia&quot; literally means &quot;Howling Arts&quot;), and the theories of Sigmund Freud popularized the art among the occult and desperate set. Most of the new adherents died due to Freud being kind of an idiot about the actual composition of the subconscious mind (the mazkim is indeed a monster from the id, but human instincts contain the altruistic and self-destructive forms; more than one mazkim has come to believe its Howler poses a threat to their loved ones by treating it as an evil beast), but the survivors adapted, and refined the art. It's not a safe magic, or a sane one - but it's open to anyone willing to stare into the abyss and find something useful, and without Nobles, often it's the best possible option. Of course, that doesn't mean the Howlers can't become evil all on their own; a few have learned to spawn Amanojaku loyal to them from their mazkim. I figure of the two examples, one is an antagonist who sends her Amanojaku to possess people she regards as a threat to her suburb, making it a shiny, happy police state run by a cult of would be neighborhood watchmen who fear <i>anything </i>that speaks of the Darkness - even Radiants, since they think a Princess draws the Dark out, when they're fine keeping it asleep.</div> </div> </div> </div>Okay.... I love both your ideas. The first provide an interesting take on an antagonist Princess, while the second allows us to both expand the lore of the Kingdom AND add a new kind of potential ally/antagonist. Feel free to add both of these to the document (the link on the first page should give you the right to edit it).<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11745-darinas","name":"Darinas","avatar":"